</style></head><body><p class="meta">APM Digital Workplace — Job Seeker Kiosk (Interim Thick-Client Solution)</p>
<h1>Conditional Access Policy Set</h1>
<p class="meta">Version 0.1 draft &nbsp;•&nbsp; 20 July 2026 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Prepared by the Twiki Corp project team</p>
<h2>Purpose</h2>
<p>The interim kiosk solution signs in with fixed per-device usernames and passwords, so kiosk identities cannot satisfy multi-factor authentication. This policy set removes MFA for the kiosk population only and replaces it with layered compensating controls: access is bound to known, compliant, Entra-joined kiosk hardware, at APM sites, on approved applications only, with every credential-theft pathway (legacy auth, device-code phishing, off-site replay) explicitly blocked.</p>
<p>All policies target the kiosk user group <code>SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</code>. Device conditions use a device extension attribute (<code>extensionAttribute1 = "KioskPlanB"</code>) stamped on kiosk device objects, because Conditional Access device filters cannot reference an Entra security group. The device group <code>SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Devices</code> continues to drive Intune policy assignment. APM sites do not present dedicated egress IP ranges, so no location-based policy is used; device binding (policies 1 and 2) carries the compensating role a trusted-location control would otherwise provide.</p>
<div class="note"><p><b>Deployment note:</b> build every policy in <b>report-only</b> mode first, confirm expected sign-in outcomes in the Entra sign-in logs, then enable. Exclude the kiosk user group from any tenant-wide MFA policy, and exclude the break-glass accounts from all policies below, per APM CA standards.</p></div>
<h2>Policies</h2><h3>1&nbsp;&nbsp;CA-APM-KioskPB-RequireCompliantDevice</h3><p class="fn">Grant — require compliant, Entra-joined kiosk device</p><p>Allows kiosk sign-in only from devices that are Entra ID joined, carry the kiosk device attribute, and are marked compliant by Intune. A valid username and password is useless on any other machine: the sign-in cannot present a compliant kiosk device claim. Session controls stop tokens outliving the working day and prevent the browser keeping the user signed in after the session ends.</p><table class="cfg"><tr><td class="k">Users</td><td>Include SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Resources</td><td>All resources</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Conditions</td><td>Platforms: Windows. Device filter (include): <code>device.trustType -eq "AzureAD" -and device.extensionAttribute1 -eq "KioskPlanB"</code></td></tr><tr><td class="k">Grant</td><td>Require device to be marked as compliant</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Session</td><td>Sign-in frequency 12 hours; persistent browser session: never persistent</td></tr></table><h3>2&nbsp;&nbsp;CA-APM-KioskPB-BlockNonKioskDevices</h3><p class="fn">Block — deny all non-kiosk devices</p><p>The explicit deny that closes the gap left by policy 1: it blocks kiosk identities on every device that is <i>not</i> a tagged kiosk — including unregistered and unknown devices, which present no attribute at all and therefore match the filter. Together, policies 1 and 2 hard-bind each kiosk identity to APM kiosk hardware.</p><table class="cfg"><tr><td class="k">Users</td><td>Include SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Resources</td><td>All resources</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Conditions</td><td>Device filter (include): <code>device.extensionAttribute1 -ne "KioskPlanB"</code></td></tr><tr><td class="k">Grant</td><td>Block access</td></tr></table><h3>3&nbsp;&nbsp;CA-APM-KioskPB-BlockNonWindows</h3><p class="fn">Block — deny non-Windows platforms</p><p>Backstop to policy 2: denies kiosk sign-ins from Android, iOS, macOS and Linux outright. Platform detection is evaluated independently of device filters, so a spoofed or anomalous device record still fails on platform.</p><table class="cfg"><tr><td class="k">Users</td><td>Include SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Resources</td><td>All resources</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Conditions</td><td>Platforms (include): Android, iOS, macOS, Linux</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Grant</td><td>Block access</td></tr></table><h3>4&nbsp;&nbsp;CA-APM-KioskPB-BlockLegacyAuth</h3><p class="fn">Block — legacy authentication</p><p>Legacy protocols (IMAP, POP, SMTP AUTH, Exchange ActiveSync) do not carry device claims, so they bypass every device-based control above. For password-only accounts this is the classic attack path; it is closed unconditionally.</p><table class="cfg"><tr><td class="k">Users</td><td>Include SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Resources</td><td>All resources</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Conditions</td><td>Client apps: Exchange ActiveSync clients, Other clients</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Grant</td><td>Block access</td></tr></table><h3>5&nbsp;&nbsp;CA-APM-KioskPB-BlockAuthFlows</h3><p class="fn">Block — device code flow and authentication transfer</p><p>Device-code phishing is the leading technique for stealing sessions from fixed-credential accounts: the attacker initiates a device-code sign-in and lures the user (or an automated kiosk flow) into approving it elsewhere. Authentication transfer similarly moves a session to an attacker-controlled device. Neither flow has any legitimate use on a kiosk.</p><table class="cfg"><tr><td class="k">Users</td><td>Include SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Resources</td><td>All resources</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Conditions</td><td>Authentication flows: device code flow, authentication transfer</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Grant</td><td>Block access</td></tr></table><h3>6&nbsp;&nbsp;CA-APM-KioskPB-BlockRiskySignIn</h3><p class="fn">Block — sign-in risk medium and above</p><p>Identity Protection blocks anomalous sign-ins (impossible travel, anonymised IP, token anomalies) at medium or high risk. The tenant standard remediates risk with MFA; kiosk accounts can never satisfy that, so the kiosk policy blocks outright and at a lower threshold. Requires Entra ID P2 coverage for the kiosk identities — confirm licensing position with SoftwareOne.</p><table class="cfg"><tr><td class="k">Users</td><td>Include SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Resources</td><td>All resources</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Conditions</td><td>Sign-in risk: medium, high</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Grant</td><td>Block access</td></tr></table><h3>7&nbsp;&nbsp;CA-APM-KioskPB-BlockUnapprovedApps</h3><p class="fn">Block — default-deny all applications except the resume surface</p><p>Kiosk identities exist for one task: creating and editing resumes in Office for the web. This policy blocks every cloud application by default and excludes only the services that task requires — Office for the web and SharePoint Online (OneDrive authenticates as SharePoint Online; it is required for Office web sessions and for staging USB files). Exchange, Teams and all other workloads are denied by default rather than by individual block policies. Data-plane exposure created by allowing SharePoint/OneDrive is controlled by Purview Information Barriers and the scratch-OneDrive wipe process, documented in the DDD §7.2.</p><table class="cfg"><tr><td class="k">Users</td><td>Include SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Resources</td><td>Include all resources; exclude Microsoft Office Online, Office 365 SharePoint Online (add Office 365 portal only if report-only testing shows the sign-in entry flow requires it)</td></tr><tr><td class="k">Grant</td><td>Block access</td></tr></table><h2>How the policies work together</h2>
<p>No single policy carries the design. Each policy addresses a distinct control layer, and access is granted only where every layer is satisfied concurrently: approved hardware, a modern authentication client, approved applications, and normal sign-in behaviour.</p>
<table><tr><th width="24%">Policy</th><th width="16%">Control layer</th><th>Function in the environment</th><th width="26%">MFA-removal role</th></tr><tr><td>1 RequireCompliantDevice</td><td>Device trust and health</td><td>Restricts access to Entra-joined, Intune-managed kiosk devices assessed as compliant</td><td>Substitutes the possession factor with the managed kiosk device</td></tr><tr><td>2 BlockNonKioskDevices</td><td>Device identity</td><td>Denies kiosk identities on any device not carrying the kiosk device attribute, including unregistered devices</td><td>Renders stolen credentials unusable on non-kiosk hardware</td></tr><tr><td>3 BlockNonWindows</td><td>Platform</td><td>Denies sign-ins from Android, iOS, macOS and Linux platforms</td><td>Backstop where device claims are absent or spoofed</td></tr><tr><td>4 BlockLegacyAuth</td><td>Protocol</td><td>Denies legacy authentication protocols that bypass device-based evaluation</td><td>Closes the password-spray attack path</td></tr><tr><td>5 BlockAuthFlows</td><td>Authentication flow</td><td>Denies device code flow and authentication transfer</td><td>Closes device-code phishing and session-transfer vectors</td></tr><tr><td>6 BlockRiskySignIn</td><td>Sign-in risk</td><td>Blocks sign-ins assessed by Identity Protection at medium risk or above</td><td>Provides the anomaly response MFA challenges would otherwise deliver</td></tr><tr><td>7 BlockUnapprovedApps</td><td>Application</td><td>Denies all cloud applications except Office for the web and SharePoint Online</td><td>Constrains the blast radius of any compromised session</td></tr></table>
<h2>Supporting controls outside Conditional Access</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Security-info registration block</b> — kiosk accounts must be prevented from registering MFA/SSPR methods, so an attacker holding the password cannot enrol their own authenticator.</li>
<li><b>Tenant MFA exclusions</b> — exclude <code>SG-APM-Kiosk-PlanB-Users</code> from tenant-wide MFA policies; verify no "all users" policy demands a control these accounts cannot meet.</li>
<li><b>Credential hygiene</b> — Key Vault-managed per-device passwords with scheduled rotation; Entra smart lockout and banned-password list apply.</li>
<li><b>Data plane</b> — Purview Information Barriers isolate the kiosk segment from all staff content in SharePoint and OneDrive; scratch OneDrive wiped at logoff plus scheduled sweep.</li>
<li><b>Device plane</b> — Assigned Access single-app lockdown (Edge + Zscaler + File Explorer), Intune compliance policy, BitLocker, and Zscaler client for web filtering.</li>
</ul></body></html>